  "raw_text": "Step 1: Since \\( p(x) \\) is a quadratic polynomial with integer coefficients and \\( 4 - \\sqrt{11} \\) is a root, its conjugate \\( 4 + \\sqrt{11} \\) must also be a root. Therefore, we can express \\( p(x) \\) as:\n\\[ p(x) = a(x - (4 - \\sqrt{11}))(x - (4 + \\sqrt{11})) \\]\nfor some integer \\( a \\).\n\nStep 2: Simplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\\[ p(x) = a((x - 4) + \\sqrt{11})((x - 4) - \\sqrt{11}) = a((x - 4)^2 - (\\sqrt{11})^2) = a((x - 4)^2 - 11) = a(x^2 - 8x + 16 - 11) = a(x^2 - 8x + 5). \\]\n\nStep 3: To find \\( \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} \\), substitute \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( x = 4 \\) into the polynomial:\n\\[ p(3) = a(3^2 - 8 \\cdot 3 + 5) = a(9 - 24 + 5) = a(-10), \\]\n\\[ p(4) = a(4^2 - 8 \\cdot 4 + 5) = a(16 - 32 + 5) = a(-11). \\]\n\nStep 4: Compute the ratio:\n\\[ \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} = \\frac{a(-10)}{a(-11)} = \\frac{-10}{-11} = \\frac{10}{11}. \\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{\\frac{10}{11}}<|im_end|>",
